{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2033 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a732be4eff6cc79e2d672b5ab6a58286cdbad869606d4ecb2118ff4764a66a4a" :edb/db-before-t 2032 :edb/db-after-t 2033 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535345 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:30:24.074Z" 13194139535345 true] [17592186133398 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2$. According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]$?" 13194139535345 false] [17592186133398 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2.$ According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]?$" 13194139535345 true] [17592186133398 1145 "Let's start by plotting the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/02c989a33b6f027c0814d39d1468379dcb24f90536257b2b0bf4031191a73f85.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,6],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(6).$\n\nSince $f(2)=3$ and $f(6)=11$, we must have \n$k\\in[3,11].$\n\n**Watch out!** According to the graph of this particular function, $f(x)=k$ may have solutions for $k < 3,$ but these are *not* guaranteed by the intermediate value theorem. The theorem only guarantees the solutions for $k$ between $f(a)$ and $f(b).$" 13194139535345 true] [17592186133398 1145 "Let's start by plotting the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2150/Source/Images/0d850b5400cf74888676e94e8c489eff.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,6]$, so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nhas a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(6)$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(2)=3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(6)=11\n$$\n\n, we must have\n$k\\in[3,11]$.\n\n**Watch out!** According to the graph of this particular function,\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nmay have solutions for $k < 3$, but these are *not* guaranteed by the intermediate value theorem. The theorem only guarantees the solutions for $k$ between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$." 13194139535345 false] [17592186133399 1100 "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2]$. Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2$?" 13194139535345 false] [17592186133399 1100 "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2?$" 13194139535345 true] [17592186133399 1145 "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-2,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-2,2]$.\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-2,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-2},{\\color{blue}-1}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}-2})={\\color{red}4}$ and $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$. Since $-2 \\not \\in [{\\color{red}-1},{\\color{red}4}]$, the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-1},{\\color{blue}0}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$ and $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$. Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}-1}]$, the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{blue}1}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$ and $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$. Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}5}]$, the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}1},{\\color{blue}2}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$ and $g({\\color{blue}2})={\\color{red}3}$. Since $-2 \\not \\in[{\\color{red}3},{\\color{red}5}]$, the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1]$. Therefore, $g(x)=-2$ has at least two solutions." 13194139535345 false] [17592186133399 1145 "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-2,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-2,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-2,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-2},{\\color{blue}-1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-2})={\\color{red}4}$ and $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in [{\\color{red}-1},{\\color{red}4}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-1},{\\color{blue}0}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$ and $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}-1}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{blue}1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$ and $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}1},{\\color{blue}2}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$ and $g({\\color{blue}2})={\\color{red}3}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in[{\\color{red}3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1].$ Therefore, $g(x)=-2$ has at least two solutions." 13194139535345 true] [17592186133402 1100 "On which type of closed intervals can  the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function \n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2-1} \\, ?\n$$" 13194139535345 true] [17592186133402 1100 "On which type of closed intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)= \\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2-1} \\,$?" 13194139535345 false] [17592186133402 1145 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x^2-1=0$, which corresponds to $x=1$ or $x=-1$.\n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain points $x=1$ or $x=-1$.\n\nFor instance, the intervals $[2,4]$ or $[-0.5,0.5]$ are allowed, but the intervals $[-2,0]$ and $[0.5, 1.5]$ are not." 13194139535345 false] [17592186133402 1145 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x^2-1=0,$ which corresponds to $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain points $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nFor instance, the intervals $[2,4]$ or $[-0.5,0.5]$ are allowed, but the intervals $[-2,0]$ and $[0.5, 1.5]$ are not." 13194139535345 true] [17592186133570 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/c79e9567742a64066dfe42651f3a4e05847bd5c8b7d8ee616238010a2926c087.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $x\\in [-5,2].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\textrm{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" 13194139535345 true] [17592186133570 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $x\\in [-5,2]$. By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2150/Source/Images/6e256c36411942eac5fc5523f999c4c3.png)\n\n> *If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\textrm{________}$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k$.*" 13194139535345 false] [17592186133570 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-5,2]$, so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nhas a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-5)$ and $f(2)$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(-5)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(2)=4\n$$\n\n, we must have $k\\in[1,4]$.\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$1$ and $4$\". The complete sentence is:\n\n> *If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $4$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k$.*" 13194139535345 false] [17592186133570 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-5,2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-5)$ and $f(2).$\n\nSince $f(-5)=1$ and $f(2)=4$, we must have $k\\in[1,4].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$1$ and $4$\". The complete sentence is:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $4$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" 13194139535345 true] [17592186133755 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344963 13194139535345 true] [17592186133755 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345043 13194139535345 true] [17592186133755 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345050 13