45008 1145 "Since $f(x)$ is a polynomial, it's continuous over the real numbers. Thus, we can apply the intermediate value theorem to any of the given intervals.\n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-70$ | $0$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-5,-3],$ we have $f(-5)=-70$ and $f(-3)=0.$ Since $-5\\in [-70,0],$ the IVT does guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-3,-2],$ we have $f(-3)=0$ and $f(-2)=8.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-2,0],$ we have $f(-2)=8$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345010 13194139535345 true] [17592186345009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345011 13194139535345 true] [17592186345009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345012 13194139535345 true] [17592186345009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345013 13194139535345 true] [17592186345009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345014 13194139535345 true] [17592186345009 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535345 true] [17592186345009 1041 "selection" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345009 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345011 13194139535345 true] [17592186345009 1110 #uuid "4c476131-9a6d-5be8-bed4-8c96f813dc16" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345010 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345010 1111 #uuid "5c89ecba-2b3a-5af9-b420-83b9f1c9ce27" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345011 1034 "I only" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345011 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345011 1111 #uuid "45482e8d-300f-5066-899c-7ada6a2e9c29" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345012 1034 "III only" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345012 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345012 1111 #uuid "8c71113e-6167-5212-9e84-d7f3e2a1b171" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345013 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345013 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345013 1111 #uuid "5da618d3-2d1b-5826-b019-3524d0f29c75" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345014 1034 "II only" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345014 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345014 1111 #uuid "c87c726b-1a25-56f4-9a96-2cca1824ab4c" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345015 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345016 13194139535345 true] [17592186345015 1089 "q-47055" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345015 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535345 true] [17592186345015 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}.$ In which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=3$ has a solution? \n\n- $[-4,-1]$\n- $[3,5]$\n- $[-1,4]$" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345015 1113 #uuid "697ba05b-3399-5081-bfb5-548a407d6a14" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345015 1145 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=2,$ and so the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2$ \n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-4,-1],$ we have $f(-4)=0.5$ and $f(-1)=0.$ Since $3\\notin [0,0.5],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[3,5],$ we have $f(3)=4$ and $f(5)=2.$ Since $3\\in [2,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,4]$ contains the point $x=2.$ Therefore we can not apply the intermediate value theorem on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\"" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345016 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345017 13194139535345 true] [17592186345016 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345018 13194139535345 true] [17592186345016 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345019 13194139535345 true] [17592186345016 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345020 13194139535345 true] [17592186345016 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345021 13194139535345 true] [17592186345016 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535345 true] [17592186345016 1041 "selection" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345016 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345021 13194139535345 true] [17592186345016 1110 #uuid "7b333f02-6466-58f8-9986-a863aba1c44b" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345017 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345017 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345017 1111 #uuid "1adffa92-2a25-54f0-a38d-b3db45a7d65c" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345018 1034 "I only" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345018 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345018 1111 #uuid "6f411143-f108-53f2-85c4-09d79aa535f2" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345019 1034 "III only" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345019 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345019 1111 #uuid "ad8eb979-e97f-5324-9249-e882c3f93a3a" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345020 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345020 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345020 1111 #uuid "cd90d2dc-2192-55f8-9f49-2009b262e204" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345021 1034 "II only" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345021 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345021 1111 #uuid "64ac0818-2e3b-543b-a8cd-d46bd1c275c9" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345022 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345023 13194139535345 true] [17592186345022 1089 "q-47056" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345022 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535345 true] [17592186345022 1100 "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-4,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345022 1113 #uuid "8186883d-213b-5f00-9b7b-9a8de4d9c22e" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345022 1145 "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-4,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-4,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-4,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1.$\n\n- On the subinterval $[-4,-3],$ we have $g(-4)=2$ and $g(-3)=-1.$ Since $1\\in [-1,2],$  the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-3,-1],$ we have $g(-3)=-1$ and $g(-1)=-5.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $g(-1)=-5$ and $g(0)=2.$ Since $1\\in [-5,2],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[0,1],$ we have $g(0)=2$ and $g(1)=3.$ Since $1\\notin [2,3],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=3$ and $g(2)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-4,-3],$ $[-1,0],$  and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least three solutions on $[-4,2].$" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345023 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345024 13194139535345 true] [17592186345023 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345025 13194139535345 true] [17592186345023 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345026 13194139535345 true] [17592186345023 1000 #edb/ref 1759218634