5064 1113 #uuid "78f4f066-a6ea-5590-a2c5-0ffdcc3f7ecb" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345064 1145 "The intermediate value theorem states the following.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is **continuous** on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(a)$ **and** $f(b)$ **inclusively**, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II\"." 13194139535345 true] [17592186345065 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345066 13194139535345 true] [17592186345065 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345067 13194139535345 true] [17592186345065 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345068 13194139535345 true] [17592186345065 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345069 13194139535345 true] [17592186345065 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345070 13194139535345 true] [17592186345065 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535345 true] [17592186345065 1041 "selection" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345065 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345070 13194139535345 true] [17592186345065 1110 #uuid "a02ee324-a714-50cd-9690-cb1111b82ecb" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345066 1034 "II, III" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345066 1111 #uuid "a3e7a00a-d4ec-585a-9d57-81f945bad7a0" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345067 1034 "I, III" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345067 1111 #uuid "2456ac11-e89b-51d6-8093-6004f02adc8a" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345068 1034 "II, I" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345068 1111 #uuid "b3f19b5f-bb77-5c93-8d82-95b55f55caf0" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345069 1034 "III, II" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345069 1111 #uuid "6d43b8c1-c699-5951-80f5-98d285b65b76" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345070 1034 "I, II" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345070 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535345 true] [17592186345070 1111 #uuid "fdba5118-c578-5d61-a859-667b43543448" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345071 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345072 13194139535345 true] [17592186345071 1089 "q-78510" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345071 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535345 true] [17592186345071 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=8-(x+2)^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-2,1]?$" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345071 1113 #uuid "4fb52c76-7e7e-5fff-a905-fce89cd57965" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345071 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,1],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(1).$\n\nSince $f(-2)=8$ and $f(1)=-1,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-1,8].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=5$ belongs to the interval $[-1,8].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/4b9b0cecb305a49f98b7b7a6a2ebca69c1ce10acdb72cb88d805e0f1901dc882.png)" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345072 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345073 13194139535345 true] [17592186345072 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345074 13194139535345 true] [17592186345072 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345075 13194139535345 true] [17592186345072 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345076 13194139535345 true] [17592186345072 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345077 13194139535345 true] [17592186345072 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535345 true] [17592186345072 1041 "selection" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345072 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345074 13194139535345 true] [17592186345072 1110 #uuid "858dae43-c314-54fe-93dd-7bad6af7bf05" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345073 1034 "k=-2" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535345 true] [17592186345073 1111 #uuid "e854a031-024a-5623-8749-d16f08921493" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345074 1034 "k=5" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535345 true] [17592186345074 1111 #uuid "2127dff1-25ba-5de5-a883-1cf12d569b51" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345075 1034 "k=10" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535345 true] [17592186345075 1111 #uuid "fbae1afc-7521-5345-811c-6a890bc02c8f" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345076 1034 "k=9" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345076 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535345 true] [17592186345076 1111 #uuid "e6de78da-bb34-5669-b5e8-0071290e17a4" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345077 1034 "k=-3" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345077 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535345 true] [17592186345077 1111 #uuid "e1406abd-fde3-5dd7-80b2-2353e6ae804c" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345078 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345079 13194139535345 true] [17592186345078 1089 "q-78537" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345078 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535345 true] [17592186345078 1100 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [2,7].$ Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x)=2?$" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345078 1113 #uuid "c832a0ab-c8ed-58dd-a542-b7df2a173528" 13194139535345 true] [17592186345078 1145 "Since $f$ is a continuous function on $[2,7]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[2,7].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[2,7]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,3],$ we have $f(2)=3$ and $f(3)=1.$ Since $2\\in [1,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,6],$ we have $f(3)=1$ and $f(6)=4.$ Since $2\\in [1,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[6,7],$ we have $f(6)=4$ and $f(7)=0.$ Since $2\\i